WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:07.000
Speaker 1: Well, it has been a very consequential
24 hours for Donald Trump, for the Justice

00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:09.930
Department, for the January 6th Committee.

00:00:09.930 --> 00:00:14.880
We have so many pieces of breaking news to
talk about, and I want to start first and

00:00:14.880 --> 00:00:23.150
foremost with a federal judge's opinion that
Donald Trump likely committed multiple felonies

00:00:23.150 --> 00:00:26.689
in trying to overturn the results of the 2020
election.

00:00:26.689 --> 00:00:29.870
Now we're going to talk about what this means
and what it doesn't mean.

00:00:29.870 --> 00:00:35.250
And many of you rightly writing to me correctly,
writing to me and saying, David, wait, a judge

00:00:35.250 --> 00:00:37.880
determines whether Trump is guilty.

00:00:37.880 --> 00:00:39.690
Doesn't a jury do that?

00:00:39.690 --> 00:00:44.170
This is not a determination of criminality
for the purposes of punishment.

00:00:44.170 --> 00:00:46.060
Donald Trump has not been charged.

00:00:46.060 --> 00:00:49.270
The context here is evidence.

00:00:49.270 --> 00:00:58.699
And does there appear to be a legitimate reason
to turn over certain records on the basis

00:00:58.699 --> 00:01:04.779
that it seems reasonable that Donald Trump
committed crimes and thus the evidence is

00:01:04.779 --> 00:01:07.220
legitimate to be looked at by investigators?

00:01:07.220 --> 00:01:11.520
And we're going to talk about that, and we
have some very specific comments about this

00:01:11.520 --> 00:01:12.659
from legal experts.

00:01:12.659 --> 00:01:14.810
Let's start with the New York Times article.

00:01:14.810 --> 00:01:21.279
Federal judge finds Trump most likely committed
crimes over the 2020 election, the judge wrote

00:01:21.279 --> 00:01:25.799
in a civil case the illegality of the plan
was obvious.

00:01:25.799 --> 00:01:31.819
Separately, the January six panel voted to
recommend contempt of Congress charges for

00:01:31.819 --> 00:01:34.670
two former Trump aides.

00:01:34.670 --> 00:01:37.459
This article is worth reading in its entirety.

00:01:37.459 --> 00:01:38.579
The important points here.

00:01:38.579 --> 00:01:43.349
A federal judge ruled yesterday that Trump
and a lawyer who advised him on how to overturn

00:01:43.349 --> 00:01:49.090
the 2020 election most likely had committed
felonies, including obstructing the work of

00:01:49.090 --> 00:01:52.859
Congress and conspiring to defraud the United
States.

00:01:52.859 --> 00:01:57.789
The judge's comments on the civil case of
the lawyer, John Eastman, marked a significant

00:01:57.789 --> 00:02:01.249
breakthrough for the House Committee investigating
the riots.

00:02:01.249 --> 00:02:07.149
The committee is weighing making a criminal
referral to the Justice Department had used

00:02:07.149 --> 00:02:12.900
a filing in the case to lay out the crimes
it believed Trump might have committed.

00:02:12.900 --> 00:02:16.310
Importantly, Trump has not been charged with
any crime.

00:02:16.310 --> 00:02:22.030
The judge's ruling has no immediate, practical
legal effect on him, but it ratifies the committee's

00:02:22.030 --> 00:02:28.829
argument that Trump's efforts to block Congress
from certifying Biden's win could well rise

00:02:28.829 --> 00:02:31.599
to the level of a criminal conspiracy.

00:02:31.599 --> 00:02:34.459
This is, we can't let this be normalized.

00:02:34.459 --> 00:02:38.489
It is outrageous that this is what is going
on.

00:02:38.489 --> 00:02:44.140
Judge David Carter wrote quote the illegality
of the plan was obvious.

00:02:44.140 --> 00:02:48.989
Our nation was founded on the peaceful transition
of power epitomized by George Washington,

00:02:48.989 --> 00:02:53.079
laying down his sword to make way for democratic
elections.

00:02:53.079 --> 00:02:58.659
Ignoring this history, President Trump vigorously
campaigned for the vice president to single

00:02:58.659 --> 00:03:02.530
handedly determine the results of the 2020
election.

00:03:02.530 --> 00:03:04.209
So what are our questions here?

00:03:04.209 --> 00:03:11.769
Well, the first question is, does this finally
give the Attorney General, Merrick Garland,

00:03:11.769 --> 00:03:15.209
a way to move forward with the criminal charges?

00:03:15.209 --> 00:03:19.430
And the answer is it probably does, but he
probably won't.

00:03:19.430 --> 00:03:24.730
Now what's really important to understand
here is what does this mean?

00:03:24.730 --> 00:03:30.400
This is not a judge ruling that a crime was
committed.

00:03:30.400 --> 00:03:33.739
That's for a jury to do if charges are brought.

00:03:33.739 --> 00:03:38.489
But I asked former FBI special agent who's
now a lawyer, Asuran Gupta.

00:03:38.489 --> 00:03:40.700
She teaches at Yale Law School.

00:03:40.700 --> 00:03:44.860
I asked her about this on Twitter, and she
explained very clearly.

00:03:44.860 --> 00:03:49.859
Quote The judge's opinion is in itself evidence
of a crime.

00:03:49.859 --> 00:03:56.519
It's an evaluation of some of the evidence,
which is the grand jury's job, I'd say.

00:03:56.519 --> 00:04:02.049
At most, it suggests that a grand jury would
be able to independently find probable cause

00:04:02.049 --> 00:04:03.329
to charge.

00:04:03.329 --> 00:04:07.810
But the problem is proving it beyond a reasonable
doubt to a jury.

00:04:07.810 --> 00:04:13.819
Asia went on to add the judge may have just
been stating what was obvious from the evidence,

00:04:13.819 --> 00:04:20.220
but no doubt it's a major flag that this had
better be an active Department of Justice

00:04:20.220 --> 00:04:21.220
investigation.

00:04:21.220 --> 00:04:24.030
Dereliction of duty if it is not.

00:04:24.030 --> 00:04:28.550
So this is the main point, and we've been
talking about this for some time.

00:04:28.550 --> 00:04:30.550
I don't think anybody is arguing.

00:04:30.550 --> 00:04:33.090
Let me put it a different way.

00:04:33.090 --> 00:04:40.180
Due process holds that one is innocent until
proven guilty and due process holds that we

00:04:40.180 --> 00:04:45.500
can't say someone should be punished for a
crime until they've been found guilty of a

00:04:45.500 --> 00:04:46.500
crime.

00:04:46.500 --> 00:04:48.370
And right now, Donald Trump has not even been
charged.

00:04:48.370 --> 00:04:52.370
So we're, you know, the right loves to say
they're all about law and order, but they

00:04:52.370 --> 00:04:56.750
will immediately suspend due process when
they want someone quote locked up.

00:04:56.750 --> 00:04:57.940
Think Hillary Clinton?

00:04:57.940 --> 00:04:59.139
We're not going to do that.

00:04:59.139 --> 00:05:01.330
We're going to stick to due process.

00:05:01.330 --> 00:05:06.860
But what is abundantly clear at this point
is we have endless opinions, including now

00:05:06.860 --> 00:05:13.419
from a federal judge, that it's at least quite
plausible that felonies have been committed.

00:05:13.419 --> 00:05:14.430
And so what does that mean?

00:05:14.430 --> 00:05:18.170
It doesn't mean you throw someone in jail
before getting a guilty verdict.

00:05:18.170 --> 00:05:22.419
But it does mean there should be an active
investigation and Asuran Raghubar is completely

00:05:22.419 --> 00:05:23.550
correct about that.

00:05:23.550 --> 00:05:28.310
So this ruling affects a very specific part
of the process of investigation.

00:05:28.310 --> 00:05:30.139
It relates to a civil case.

00:05:30.139 --> 00:05:31.650
It's not time for jail.

00:05:31.650 --> 00:05:33.280
We're not saying lock anybody up.

00:05:33.280 --> 00:05:36.199
It's the right that loves to do that by skipping
due process.

00:05:36.199 --> 00:05:42.440
But what we are saying is if there's no investigation
going on right now, it raises some major major

00:05:42.440 --> 00:05:45.009
questions that we need to have answered.

00:05:45.009 --> 00:05:50.220
And unfortunately, what we saw in Manhattan
is very similar to this.

00:05:50.220 --> 00:05:55.250
Remember that Mark Pomerantz and another prosecutor
investigating Trump criminally in Manhattan

00:05:55.250 --> 00:05:57.000
resigned.

00:05:57.000 --> 00:06:01.330
And initially, many on the right were saying
they resigned because they knew that it was

00:06:01.330 --> 00:06:04.310
all bogus and there was nothing to investigate.

00:06:04.310 --> 00:06:07.340
No, we speculated, and it's since been confirmed.

00:06:07.340 --> 00:06:13.449
They resigned because they actually thought,
Wow, Trump did commit crimes, and it doesn't

00:06:13.449 --> 00:06:16.409
seem there's any interest in actually charging
him.

00:06:16.409 --> 00:06:19.759
And so our work is essentially being done
for nothing.

00:06:19.759 --> 00:06:21.979
We found evidence of criminality.

00:06:21.979 --> 00:06:24.800
Nobody's interested in charging the guy I'm
out of here.

00:06:24.800 --> 00:06:25.800
That's what happened.

00:06:25.800 --> 00:06:29.110
So we're seeing the same story.

00:06:29.110 --> 00:06:36.390
Federal judge, civil case, Manhattan investigators,
everybody coming to the conclusion felonies

00:06:36.390 --> 00:06:38.450
were probably committed here.

00:06:38.450 --> 00:06:39.889
Will he be charged?

00:06:39.889 --> 00:06:40.889
I'm not.

00:06:40.889 --> 00:08:01.169
I'm not particularly optimistic.

